      modelAnswer: 'The fetch-decode-execute cycle is the basic algorithm by which a CPU executes a stored program. Fetch. The address of the next instruction is held in the program counter (PC). The PC\'s contents are copied into the memory address register (MAR); the address bus carries this address to memory; the memory data register (MDR) receives the contents of that memory location; the instruction is then copied from the MDR into the current instruction register (CIR). The PC is incremented to point to the next instruction in sequence. Decode. The control unit examines the bit pattern in the CIR. It identifies the operation code (opcode) — what kind of instruction (add, load, store, branch) — and any operands (which registers or memory locations to use). It generates the corresponding control signals to direct the rest of the CPU. Execute. The control signals trigger the operation. For arithmetic the ALU performs the calculation on the specified registers and writes the result back; for a load, data is fetched from memory; for a store, data is written to memory; for a branch, the PC is overwritten with the target address. The cycle then begins again. Modern CPUs overlap many of these steps (pipelining), execute multiple instructions per cycle (superscalar) and reorder them (out-of-order execution), but the underlying cycle remains fetch, decode, execute, repeat.'
